1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,502
Chai Corner has six people now.

2
00:00:02,802 --> 00:00:05,531
But every cup of chai
is made a different way.

3
00:00:06,081 --> 00:00:07,908
One cup takes two minutes.

4
00:00:07,908 --> 00:00:09,271
Another takes five.

5
00:00:09,571 --> 00:00:11,820
Is there one best way to make chai?

6
00:00:12,470 --> 00:00:13,865
Ravi stops guessing.

7
00:00:13,865 --> 00:00:15,261
He starts measuring.

8
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:19,045
This is the big idea of
scientific management.

9
00:00:19,345 --> 00:00:23,095
It comes from Frederick Winslow
Taylor, an American engineer.

10
00:00:23,445 --> 00:00:26,019
He is the father of scientific management.

11
00:00:26,369 --> 00:00:31,496
In 1911, he wrote the book, The
Principles of Scientific Management.

12
00:00:31,846 --> 00:00:35,085
Study each job, and find
the one best way to do it.

13
00:00:35,735 --> 00:00:39,300
His most famous study was at
Bethlehem Steel, in America.

14
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:44,031
Workers shovelled coal and iron ore
all day, each with his own shovel.

15
00:00:44,381 --> 00:00:46,384
Taylor tested load after load.

16
00:00:46,384 --> 00:00:49,197
The best scoop was
about twenty one pounds.

17
00:00:49,547 --> 00:00:53,536
So each material got its own
shovel, sized to carry that load.

18
00:00:53,886 --> 00:00:58,157
Each man now moved about fifty
nine tons a day, up from sixteen.

19
00:00:58,807 --> 00:01:01,087
Ravi tries the same thing with chai.

20
00:01:01,387 --> 00:01:03,873
He times every step with a stopwatch.

21
00:01:03,873 --> 00:01:06,516
This is called a time and motion study.

22
00:01:06,866 --> 00:01:10,059
Asha keeps the milk, tea
and sugar within reach.

23
00:01:10,059 --> 00:01:11,253
No wasted steps.

24
00:01:11,603 --> 00:01:14,213
A cup that took five
minutes now takes three.

25
00:01:14,863 --> 00:01:17,559
Taylor built all of
this on four principles.

26
00:01:17,909 --> 00:01:19,818
Science, not rule of thumb.

27
00:01:19,818 --> 00:01:21,774
Rule of thumb means guesswork.

28
00:01:22,124 --> 00:01:23,764
Harmony, not discord.

29
00:01:23,764 --> 00:01:26,368
Managers and workers trust each other.

30
00:01:26,718 --> 00:01:28,936
Cooperation, not individualism.

31
00:01:28,936 --> 00:01:31,496
Everyone works together, not alone.

32
00:01:31,846 --> 00:01:35,405
And develop every worker, so
each person reaches their best.

33
00:01:36,055 --> 00:01:38,821
He also gave managers a few more tools.

34
00:01:39,171 --> 00:01:40,304
Standardisation.

35
00:01:40,304 --> 00:01:43,609
The same tools and the
same recipe, every time.

36
00:01:43,959 --> 00:01:45,872
Scientific selection and training.

37
00:01:45,872 --> 00:01:48,517
Pick the right person,
then train them well.

38
00:01:48,867 --> 00:01:50,305
Differential piece rate.

39
00:01:50,305 --> 00:01:53,303
Hit the target, and earn
a higher rate per piece.

40
00:01:53,653 --> 00:01:55,456
And functional foremanship.

41
00:01:55,456 --> 00:01:57,847
Eight expert bosses instead of one.

42
00:01:58,497 --> 00:01:59,655
Output went up.

43
00:01:59,655 --> 00:02:03,022
But critics said Taylor
treated workers like machines.

44
00:02:03,372 --> 00:02:07,161
The same small task, all day,
made work dull and tiring.

45
00:02:07,511 --> 00:02:11,539
So Ravi keeps the best method,
but he also listens to his team.

46
00:02:12,189 --> 00:02:13,129
Quick check.

47
00:02:13,129 --> 00:02:16,269
Every helper uses the
same pot, cup and recipe.

48
00:02:16,269 --> 00:02:17,906
Which technique is this?

49
00:02:19,806 --> 00:02:20,912
Standardisation!

50
00:02:20,912 --> 00:02:24,549
The same tools and the same
method, every single time.

51
00:02:25,149 --> 00:02:26,158
So, remember.

52
00:02:26,458 --> 00:02:30,637
Scientific management means finding
the one best way, by measuring.

53
00:02:30,987 --> 00:02:35,249
Taylor's tools include time study,
standard methods and piece rates.

54
00:02:35,599 --> 00:02:37,430
But people are not machines.

55
00:02:37,430 --> 00:02:38,735
Care for them too.

56
00:02:39,335 --> 00:02:44,172
Next time, we'll meet Henri Fayol, and
his rules for managing a whole company.
